at 807 on the morning of January 13 2018  Hawaii residents were woken up by the  following sound along with this message  ballistic missile threat inbound to  Hawaii  seek immediate shelter  this is not a drill  similar alerts interrupted TV and radio  stations leading many to hide in closets  pull off to the side of the road and  even call to say goodbye to loved ones  for the next 38 minutes 1.4 million  people contemplated for the first time  with any Gravity the Hopeless  inescapability of nuclear missiles  2300 miles and half an ocean from the  nearest major city Hawaiians simply have  nowhere to hide  but the truth is neither do any of us  Ronald Reagan made the same solemn  Discovery over 40 years ago when he  visited the Cheyenne Mountain nuclear  bunker outside of Colorado Springs  today the bunker at Cheyenne Mountain is  part of the United States space force  the youngest of the country's six  branches of armed forces  but back then it was run by NORAD which  you might recognize from its annual  tradition of tracking Santa's sleigh  NORAD was and is a real organization the  North American Aerospace Defense command  and it was set up to monitor attacks  from the skies  if the Soviets had ever sent a missile  hurling towards San Francisco it was  NORAD stationed here at Cheyenne  Mountain that would see it coming and  alert the president  all this amazed Reagan who behind a  reinforced steel door listened to  briefings on how the system worked  its giant computer screens and Hollywood  reminiscent flashing Maps gave visitors  a distinct feeling of reassurance  here science fiction was reality  but then Reagan asked the commander a  simple question  what could they actually do besides  simply retaliate after the Soviets fired  a missile  the answer deeply Disturbed him  nothing  when NORAD was created in the 50s it was  designed to help intercept bombers but  by the time of Reagan's visit the  primary threat had shifted to missiles  leaving NORAD the task of merely  watching as residents of Hawaii thought  they were the end of the world  few Americans are aware how true that  fact remains today 40 years later in  2022  while the enemy has changed more than  once since Reagan's time the basic fact  has not the United States still does not  have an effective defense against  incoming missiles  read between the lines and not even the  Department of Defense claims to have any  protection besides the threat of  retaliation against the likes of Russia  or China  but what no politician or Pentagon  official will dare say out loud is that  we aren't even defended from a much  lesser threat like North Korea or Iran  in all technical likelihood Kim Jong-un  could flatten any American city of his  choice at any time  while the Hawaii missile alert was sent  by human error the truth is that had  North Korea launched a mere fraction of  its nuclear stockpile it's all but  guaranteed that at least one would reach  its Target  It Could Happen by accident by breach of  protocol through a misunderstanding by  an irrational actor or in theory a  terrorist plot  but even fewer Americans are aware of  how many billions we've spent trying to  change this with very little success  the military is no stranger to wasteful  white elephants or bloated budgets to  say the least yet the scale of our  missile defense efforts would surprise  even the most hardened critic of the  military-industrial complex  it's one of the most expensive defense  projects ever undertaken though it's  produced far less than almost anyone is  aware of and certainly less than most  who are will admit  sponsored by brilliant learn math  science and computer science the  intuitive way with the link in the  description  if America was attacked from afar it  would be with a strategic ballistic  missile  strategic as opposed to Tactical because  it would be launched from a distance and  Ballistic rather than Cruise because it  would strike a fixed predetermined  Target like a city or military base  its trajectory would follow three  distinct phases  first during the Boost phase a rocket  slowly propels the missile upwards  producing a bright plume of exhaust  the mid-course phase begins when the  rocket stops burning allowing the  missile to fly toward its Target  finally as the powerful force of gravity  pulls it back down to earth it enters  the very short terminal phase  now all else being equal if you were  trying to shoot down this incoming  missile you'd want to do it as early as  possible  not only does it travel slowest during  the Boost phase but the burning rocket  makes it easiest to spot plus if worse  comes to worst and your first attempt  fails you might have enough time to try  again  realistically however intercepting a  ballistic missile during its boost phase  is usually just plain impossible  take for example North Korea  if a Hua song 15 missile were launched  at Boston this is roughly the path it  would take  according to the American physical  Society it would have to be intercepted  within the first 260 seconds which would  place it about here  for our defending missile to get there  in time it would have to be launched  from somewhere inside this circle  in other words we would have to  permanently station missiles above or  within either North Korean Chinese or  Russian territory  so too would the actual Collision occur  above one of those three countries  sure it could be intercepted earlier  allowing our missiles to be launched  from anywhere in here but that would  leave mere seconds for the incoming  missile to be detected for the president  to be informed and for a decision to be  made about whether and how to respond  if North Korea were kind enough to  attack further south say Hawaii or San  Francisco American launchers could  theoretically be stationed in South  Korea Japan or international waters  but that would leave them well within  range of a quick preemptive attack  we do on the other hand have examples of  successful mid-course and terminal phase  intercepts  Israel's Iron Dome has demonstrated  roughly 90 percent real world  Effectiveness and Iraqi scud missiles  were shot down during the Gulf War  we're pretty good at intercepting short  medium and even intermediate range  missiles under about 2500 kilometers or  1500 miles  overseas American military bases and  several of our allies like South Korea  and Romania are relatively well  protected from their neighbors sometimes  even by overlapping defenses  though even they aren't perfect the iron  domes 90 track record is only for the 50  percent of rockets it chooses to engage  in The Patriot missiles used in the Gulf  War were discovered to be far less  effective than was known at the time  but borrowing some North Korean takeover  of Vancouver or Cancun America faces No  short medium or intermediate range  threats  any missile that would hit the United  States would be InterContinental which  is a whole different ball game  these missiles travel up to 100 times  further seven times faster and can do a  million times more damage  not only that but they go way up into  the upper atmosphere  intercepting icbms traveling at least 15  000 miles an hour requires hitting a  bullet with a bullet in space  to really be effective they even have to  strike the right part of the bullet  so can we do it  today the system that claims to protect  all 50 states from intercontinental  ballistic missiles like those from North  Korea is called ground-based mid-course  defense GMD  of its 18 tests conducted between 1999  and 2018 it failed eight giving it a  success rate of just 55 percent  of those eight failures by the way six  were for different reasons ranging from  sensor malfunctions to launch failures  not to mention the missile defense  agency's definition of success is a bit  generous  for comparison are mainly c-based  short-range defense called Aegis Works  about 81 percent of the time  thaad the land-based short-range defense  we sell to Israel the UAE South Korea  and Romania has a 100 track record  clearly intercontinental ballistic  missiles the only kind America is  vulnerable to are just much much harder  to stop  but more telling is how often GMD isn't  tested  these are all the tests actually  conducted by the missile defense agency  across all their missile defense  programs GMD thaad and Aegis whether  they were delayed or happened on  schedule  and these are all the times tests have  been postponed merged or altogether  canceled  between 2010 and 19 nearly two-thirds of  tests fit into this category  the original plan for the ground-based  mid-course defense program specifically  called for four annual tests for five  consecutive years but they have only  actually been less than one on average  despite repeated failures testing has in  fact slowed down  this does not obviously Inspire  confidence  the usual government response is hey if  one missile only has a 55 percent chance  of hitting its Target no worries we'll  just send three or four bringing the  probability that at least one does to  upwards of 95 percent  voila problem solved  but this is nowhere near as true as it  sounds and for a very specific  historical reason  the United States has been trying to  intercept ballistic missiles since the  1960s and George W bush entered office  already wanting to invest more in the  program  but 911 changed everything  suddenly there was a tremendous sense of  urgency  in 2002 Bush announced that the  ground-based mid-course defense system  which was then just a prototype with no  end to development in sight would be  declared operational in 2004. it didn't  matter in his eyes that the technology  wasn't ready for prime time because the  threat of Rogue States like North Korea  was simply too pressing  to accomplish this massive feat in a  mere two years time Bush did something  entirely unprecedented  normally the way just about anything is  produced from toaster ovens to 100  million dollar missiles is that  scientists and Engineers first developed  the core technology after which it's all  packaged together into one  interconnected product and finally it's  produced at scale  now if you really need that toaster ASAP  you might consider cutting some Corners  by for instance starting to manufacture  it while the engineers are still putting  on some finishing touches would a  project manager would call a concurrent  schedule  but after 9 11 Bush took this Hasty  approach a step further  time was of such great importance that  he started producing the missiles while  the technology was being developed  effectively doing everything at once  as one retired senior military official  admitted quote we took a system that was  still in development and it was declared  to be operational for political reasons  for every other defense project  contractors have to demonstrate their  technology before the Pentagon will pay  for it Fly before you buy  but GMD was Exempted  what this meant was that when things  would inevitably break Engineers would  have to go back and disassemble the  highly complex missiles and try to  retrofit Solutions  all of this is to say each missile is  handmade and many have been  deconstructed and redesigned  in other words there's no way of knowing  each one's actual odds of success four  missiles might be four times more  effective than one or they may not we  just don't know  what we do know is that independent  experts aren't very confident  in 2012 a study by the National Academy  of Sciences labeled the system deficient  Ten Years Later the American physical  Society not only called its current  capabilities low but said they'll likely  continue to be low for at least the next  15 years  America's own government accountability  office called its testing quote  insufficient to demonstrate that an  operationally useful defense capability  exists  AKA it doesn't work  today 21 years after 9 11 the program  continues to run in emergency mode with  the missile defense agency tasked with  both developing the technology and  supervising its own performance  now you might argue that despite these  shortcomings some defense even just 55  percent is much better than zero  but there are two problems with such an  argument  first missile defense is insanely  expensive  cost estimates for the GMD system alone  vary depending on who you ask between 40  and 90 billion dollars making it more  expensive than the Navy's latest Ford  class carrier that's an entire thousand  foot long nuclear-powered completely  redesigned aircraft carrier  this is the quintessential American  Prestige project a 3000 crew 100  aircraft floating military base that  makes most countries look weak by  comparison and yet it was cheaper than  this one ineffective missile defense  system  add up all the countries such programs  and the total cost is closer to 350  billion current inflation-adjusted  dollars  that's an insane amount of money even by  American Military standards but the  second reason is far far worse  America's real missile defense problem  is not actually that it doesn't work or  even that it's stupidly expensive but  something else entirely something so big  it deserves its own video  because we've only just scratched the  surface of which should be a scandal we  certainly haven't done the science or  engineering behind missile defense  Justice which is why in the meantime you  should check out brilliant's Hands-On  explanations of orbits Rockets  gravitational physics and so much more  these interactive lessons teach you real  useful Math Science and Engineering by  explaining answers to questions like how  much fuel does a rocket need why are  some objects in space spherical and  others potato shaped and one is the  mathematically optimal battery size for  a drone  brilliant is when I desperately wish  school was like they get that you're  naturally going to learn more when you  actuall